---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
const guide = 'https://github.com/izyuumi/yorozu/blob/projectx/docs/setup.md';
---
<Base title="Yorozu — Your Mac's assistant, from the menu bar and your iPhone" description="Yorozu is one conversation with an assistant on your own Mac. It hands real work to OpenClaw, or Hermes Agent, and reaches your iPhone end-to-end encrypted through a relay that cannot read your messages.">
  <Fragment slot="head">
    <script is:inline src="/copy.js" defer></script>
    <meta property="og:title" content="Yorozu">
    <meta property="og:description" content="One conversation with the assistant on your Mac, from the menu bar or your iPhone. End-to-end encrypted.">
    <meta property="og:url" content="https://yorozu.yumi.to/">
    <meta property="og:image" content="https://yorozu.yumi.to/assets/icon.png">
  </Fragment>
  <section class="hero" aria-labelledby="title">
    <div class="hero-inner">
      <div class="hero-copy">
        <h1 id="title">Your Mac's agent.<br><em>In your pocket.</em></h1>
        <p class="lede">Yorozu <span class="kana" lang="ja">(よろず)</span> is one conversation with an assistant that lives on your Mac. It answers quick questions itself and hands bigger work to background workers through OpenClaw, or Hermes Agent. Ask from the menu bar or your iPhone, follow each topic in its own sub-chat, and read the result wherever you are.</p>
        <div class="actions">
          <a class="button" href="/mac">Download v1 for Mac</a>
          <a class="button ghost" href="/iphone">Join v1 on TestFlight</a>
        </div>
        <p class="fine">Both buttons install Yorozu v1, the current public release. Yorozu 0.7.0, described on this page, builds from source for now: <a href={guide}>setup guide</a>.<br>Mac: macOS 15+ · iPhone: iOS 18+ · <a href="#setup-with-an-agent">Set up with an agent</a> · <a href="https://github.com/izyuumi/yorozu">Open source, MIT</a></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <style>
    /* No product screenshots until the 0.7.0 ones exist: the hero is copy only. Astro emits
       this as an external stylesheet (inlineStylesheets: 'never'), which the CSP allows. */
    .hero-inner { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); }
    .hero-copy { padding-bottom: clamp(48px, 8vw, 110px); }
  </style>

  <section class="agent-setup" id="setup-with-an-agent" aria-labelledby="agent-setup-title">
    <div class="agent-setup-text">
      <h2 id="agent-setup-title">Set it up with one prompt.</h2>
      <p>Paste this into Claude Code, Codex, OpenClaw or any coding agent on your Mac. Your agent runs <code>Yorozu setup --json</code> and asks you each of its questions: the harness, models, integrations and YOLO mode. It shows you every change before you answer, never installs software, signs in or grants permissions for you, and stops when setup says it is done.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prompt">
      <pre><code id="agent-prompt">Read https://raw.githubusercontent.com/izyuumi/yorozu/main/Start.md and follow it to set up Yorozu on my devices. Ask me before configuring anything.</code></pre>
      <button type="button" class="copy" data-copy="agent-prompt" hidden>Copy</button>
    </div>
    <p class="copy-status" id="agent-prompt-status" role="status" aria-atomic="true"></p>
    <p class="fine">The guide is <a href="https://github.com/izyuumi/yorozu/blob/main/Start.md">Start.md</a> in the repository. It needs Yorozu 0.7.0 built from source first. Prefer to do it yourself? <a href="#iphone-availability">Install</a> and <a href="#pair">pair</a> by hand. Either way, read <a href="#before-you-install">Before you install</a> first.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="more" aria-labelledby="mac-title">
    <h2 id="mac-title">It lives in your menu bar.</h2>
    <dl class="facts">
      <div><dt>A quick chat</dt><dd>Click the Yorozu icon for the main timeline and a composer. Type, or drop in a file.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Search with ⌘F</dt><dd>Find anything you have said, with next, previous and a count.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Always running</dt><dd>The icon shows when work is running or something needs you. Yorozu keeps working after you close the popover.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>A Settings window</dt><dd>General, Devices, Connection, Storage and Jobs, plus Advanced when you want it. Or change a setting by asking in the chat.</dd></div>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <section class="more" aria-labelledby="phone-title">
    <h2 id="phone-title">Your iPhone is the full client.</h2>
    <dl class="facts">
      <div><dt>A sub-chat for every topic</dt><dd>See what is running, what needs your attention and what finished, and look inside each task as it works.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Stop and Retry</dt><dd>Stop a task that went the wrong way, or retry one that failed, right from its sub-chat.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Jobs</dt><dd>See every scheduled job with its next run and last result. Pause, resume, run now or delete it.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Search everything</dt><dd>Search the full history on your Mac, sub-chats included, not just what your phone has loaded.</dd></div>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <section class="more" aria-labelledby="harness-title">
    <h2 id="harness-title">It works through the agent you already run.</h2>
    <dl class="facts">
      <div><dt>OpenClaw, or Hermes Agent</dt><dd>Yorozu drives one harness on your Mac through its local API, with the providers and models you set up there. Setup detects which one you have. Hermes Agent support is newer and has had less use than OpenClaw.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Coding when you want it</dt><dd>Coding is opt-in: choose one repository in Settings › Advanced, and the harness runs its coding executors there, such as Claude Code or Codex through OpenClaw.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Computer use, optional</dt><dd>With <a href="https://cua.ai">CuaDriver</a> installed, workers can operate apps on your Mac in the background. It is an integration you can turn off in Settings.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>YOLO mode</dt><dd>Off by default. Turn it on and workers stop asking first before steps you requested. Settings changes, actions you did not ask for and new job scripts still need your yes.</dd></div>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <section class="more" aria-labelledby="delivery-title">
    <h2 id="delivery-title">Send now, even if the Mac is asleep.</h2>
    <dl class="facts">
      <div><dt>Files, both ways</dt><dd>Attach photos, camera shots, files or anything you paste, up to 10 files of 50 MB each. Workers can hand files back with their results.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Delivery marks</dt><dd>Every message moves from Sending to Delivered to Read. One that never arrives says so, with Resend and Delete.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>A 24-hour buffer</dt><dd>While your Mac is offline, the relay holds your messages for up to 24 hours. Your phone tells you if one expires before the Mac picks it up.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Direct at home, if you like</dt><dd>Turn on Direct connection on the phone and it talks to the Mac over your LAN or Tailscale, with the relay as the fallback. It is off by default.</dd></div>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <section class="more" aria-labelledby="files-title">
    <h2 id="files-title">Yours, as plain files.</h2>
    <dl class="facts">
      <div><dt>Memory in Markdown</dt><dd>Yorozu remembers useful facts on its own and keeps them as Markdown files in <code>~/Yorozu/memory</code>. Open them in any editor.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Jobs on a schedule</dt><dd>Ask for something to run on a schedule and Yorozu's own scheduler runs it. A new or changed script runs only after you say yes to it.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Settings you can read</dt><dd>Settings live in <code>config.toml</code> and jobs in <code>jobs.toml</code>. Edit them by hand, in Settings, or by asking in the chat.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Alerts without content</dt><dd>Your phone hears about a reply, a question, a failure or the Mac coming back online. The alert carries fixed text, never your messages.</dd></div>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <section class="relay" aria-labelledby="relay-title">
    <h2 id="relay-title">The relay can't read a word of it.</h2>
    <figure class="diagram" role="img" aria-label="Your iPhone and your Mac exchange messages encrypted with ChaCha20-Poly1305 through a relay that sees only size and timing.">
      <span class="diagram-art" aria-hidden="true"></span>
      <figcaption aria-hidden="true">
        <span class="diagram-encryption">ChaCha20-Poly1305 · End-to-end encrypted</span>
        <span class="diagram-labels"><span>iPhone</span><span class="diagram-relay">sees size and timing</span><span>Mac</span></span>
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
    <dl class="facts">
      <div><dt>Keys never leave your devices</dt><dd>An X25519 exchange at pairing time. The relay stores public keys and a room id, nothing it could open.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>No Yorozu account</dt><dd>No email, no password, no analytics. Your provider logins stay in your harness, OpenClaw or Hermes Agent, on the Mac.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Run it yourself if you like</dt><dd>The relay is a few hundred lines. Put it on a Mac mini over Tailscale and set its URL in Settings › Connection. Every paired phone then pairs again.</dd></div>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <section class="iphone-availability" id="iphone-availability" aria-labelledby="iphone-title">
    <div class="availability-intro">
      <h2 id="iphone-title">Get the apps.</h2>
      <p>The download and TestFlight links install <strong>Yorozu v1</strong>, the current public release, which works differently from this page. Yorozu 0.7.0 has no installer yet. Build it from source until there is a Mac download and a public TestFlight for it.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="installation-options">
      <article>
        <h3>Yorozu 0.7.0, from source</h3>
        <p>Developers can build the 0.7.0 Mac and iPhone apps with Xcode and Tuist from the repository's <code>projectx</code> branch. The setup guide lists what you need, and the iPhone app needs your own signing team.</p>
        <a href={guide}>Open the 0.7.0 setup guide</a>
      </article>
      <article>
        <h3>Mac, v1</h3>
        <p>Installs Yorozu v1 for Apple silicon Macs. Move it to Applications and open it. Want early v1 builds? <a href="/beta">Download the v1 beta</a>.</p>
        <a href="/mac">Download v1 for Mac</a>
      </article>
      <article>
        <h3>iPhone, v1, via TestFlight</h3>
        <p>The public TestFlight installs the v1 iPhone app. Open the link on your iPhone, install <a href="https://apps.apple.com/app/testflight/id899247664">TestFlight</a> if prompted, then install Yorozu.</p>
        <a href="/iphone">Join v1 on TestFlight</a>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="steps" id="pair" aria-labelledby="steps-title">
    <h2 id="steps-title">Then pair once.</h2>
    <p class="steps-intro">These steps are for Yorozu 0.7.0. You’ll need a Mac with macOS 15 or later and <a href="https://docs.openclaw.ai/install">OpenClaw</a> or <a href="https://hermes-agent.nousresearch.com">Hermes Agent</a> set up, and an iPhone with iOS 18 or later. Keep the Mac awake and online to reach it from your phone.</p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <span class="num" aria-hidden="true">1</span>
        <h3>Set up the Mac</h3>
        <p>Open Yorozu from the menu bar. The setup window walks you through the harness, models, integrations and YOLO mode, and skips whatever is already in place.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="num" aria-hidden="true">2</span>
        <h3>Pair your iPhone</h3>
        <p>On the Mac, open Settings › Devices › Pair iPhone. Scan the code with the iPhone Camera, or open Yorozu on the iPhone, tap “Enter code manually” and paste the code. Check that the Mac key matches. No Yorozu account needed.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="num" aria-hidden="true">3</span>
        <h3>Talk from anywhere</h3>
        <p>The conversation lives on the Mac. Your phone reaches it through the relay, or directly at home if you turn that on.</p>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section class="warning" id="before-you-install" aria-labelledby="risk-title">
    <h2 id="risk-title">Before you install</h2>
    <p><strong>Using Yorozu could get your Claude or ChatGPT/Codex account suspended or banned.</strong> Your harness runs coding executors such as Claude Code and Codex, and the models behind them, with the accounts you signed in with. Anthropic and OpenAI decide what use of their subscriptions is allowed, change those rules without notice, and have suspended accounts used with third-party agent tools. If you want the lowest risk, use an API key rather than a subscription login.</p>
    <p>AI agents take real actions on your Mac: they run commands, edit and delete files, operate apps and can send messages. Review what you allow, especially with YOLO mode on. See the <a href="/terms/">Terms of Use</a>.</p>
  </section>
</Base>
